"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The surgical findings are favorable, showing no extraprostatic extension, negative surgical margins, and no seminal vesicle invasion. The preoperative PSA was 5.2. The biopsy demonstrated Gleason 3+4 in the peripheral zone, with a different Gleason 3+3 finding on the second biopsy, which introduces some histological variability. The absence of lymph nodes removed means nodal status is unknown, which widens the uncertainty regarding potential recurrence.\n\nCountervailing factors include the negative surgical margins and lack of seminal vesicle invasion. Information that would significantly revise this prognosis is the postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data prevents a definitive assessment of the current status. The uncertainty surrounding nodal sampling, as no nodes were removed, also impacts the certainty of the outcome. The fixed estimated horizon is 74.38 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 44.63–104.13 months; its coverage has not been validated."
